answer_body:
For an option with price $C$ , the P $\&$ L, with respect to changes of the underlying asset price $S$ and volatility $\sigma$ , is given by  egin{align*} P\&L = \delta \Delta S +  rac{1}{2}\gamma (\Delta S)^2 + 
u \Delta \sigma, \end{align*} where $\delta$ , $\gamma$ , and $
u$ are respectively the delta, gamma, and vega hedge ratios. Then it is clear the vega P $\&$ L has exposure to the change of the implied volatility $\sigma$ . Note that, for the gamma P $\&$ L,  egin{align*}  rac{1}{2}\gamma (\Delta S)^2 =  rac{1}{2}\gamma S^2  rac{1}{\Delta t}\left( rac{\Delta S}{S}ight)^2\Delta t, \end{align*} where $ rac{1}{\Delta t}\left( rac{\Delta S}{S}ight)^2$ is the realized variance, and $\sqrt{ rac{1}{\Delta t}\left( rac{\Delta S}{S}ight)^2}$ is the realized volatility. To see why $\sqrt{ rac{1}{\Delta t}\left( rac{\Delta S}{S}ight)^2}$ is the realized volatility, we assume that, heuristically,  egin{align*} dS_t = S_t\left(r dt + \sigma_{Re} dW_t ight), \end{align*} where $\sigma_{Re}$ is the realized volatility and $\{W_t, \, t \ge 0\}$ is a standard Brownian motion. Then  egin{align*} \sqrt{ rac{1}{\Delta t}\left( rac{\Delta S}{S}ight)^2} \approx \sigma_{Re}. \end{align*} Consider the delta neutral portfolio $\Pi=C- rac{\partial C}{\partial S}S$ . Assuming that the interest rate and volatility are not change during the small time period $\Delta t$ . The P $\&$ L of the portfolio is given by  egin{align*} P\&L_{\Delta t}^{\Pi} &=  rac{1}{2}\gamma (\Delta S)^2 + 	heta \Delta t, \end{align*} where $	heta$ is the theta hedge ratio. For small interest rate, which we assume to be zero, $	heta \approx - rac{1}{2}\gamma S^2 \sigma^2$ and $\gamma =  rac{
u}{S^2\sigma T}$ ; see, for example, Black–Scholes model . Then  egin{align*} P\&L_{\Delta t}^{\Pi} &\approx  rac{1}{2}\gamma S^2  rac{1}{\Delta t}\left( rac{\Delta S}{S}ight)^2\Delta t -  rac{1}{2}\gamma S^2 \sigma^2 \Delta t\ &\approx  rac{1}{2}\gamma S^2 \sigma_{Re}^2 \Delta t -  rac{1}{2}\gamma S^2 \sigma^2 \Delta t\ &=  rac{1}{2}\gamma S^2 (\sigma_{Re} + \sigma)(\sigma_{Re} - \sigma) \Delta t\ &\approx \gamma S^2 \sigma (\sigma_{Re} - \sigma) \Delta t \hspace{1in} (	ext{assuming that } \sigma_{Re}\approx \sigma)\ &= rac{
u}{T}(\sigma_{Re} - \sigma) \Delta t. \end{align*} The cumulative P $\&$ L, over the interval $[0, T]$ , is then $
u (\sigma_{Re} - \sigma)$ .
